        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "kimi-F1",
            "clusterKey": "scenario-policy-identity-fmt-sprint-collision",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/value_scenario.go:49",
            "summary": "Alternative-inventory change detection compares `fmt.Sprint(ids)` where ids is a sorted []string; because fmt.Sprint is not injective (e.g. [\"a\",\"b\"] and [\"a b\"] both render as \"[a b]\"), two scenarios with genuinely different alternative identities can be treated as identical, silently bypassing the inventory-change guard the doc promises.",
            "confidence": "9/10",
            "evidence": [
              "go test ./internal/optimization with a probe constructing colliding ID sets shows AssessValueScenarios does not reject the identity change",
              "value_scenario.go builds `encodedIDs := fmt.Sprint(ids)` and compares strings; an executable probe (scenario1 alternatives [\"a\",\"b\"], scenario2 [\"a b\"]) returned nil error, confirming the guard was bypassed"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F2",
            "clusterKey": "scenario-policy-identity-fmt-sprint-collision",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/value_policy.go:88",
            "summary": "Policy selection-change detection uses `fmt.Sprint(policyResult.Selected)` as the comparison key; the same non-injective rendering means two different Selected sets can compare equal when an alternative ID contains a space, so SelectionChanges can be falsely reported as false.",
            "confidence": "8/10",
            "evidence": [
              "same root cause as value_scenario.go; a crafted ID set renders distinct selections identically",
              "value_policy.go sets `selection := fmt.Sprint(policyResult.Selected)` and compares to firstSelection; fmt.Sprint on []string is not injective for space-bearing IDs"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F3",
            "clusterKey": "twin-fact-value-byte-sensitivity",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/twin.go:131",
            "summary": "BuildTwinSnapshot detects conflicting facts by comparing `string(fact.Value) != string(field.Value)` (raw bytes after json.Compact, which preserves key order), so two semantically identical JSON values with different key order are reported as CONFLICTING rather than KNOWN.",
            "confidence": "9/10",
            "evidence": [
              "json.Compact in NewTemporalFact does not canonicalize object key order, so conflict detection is byte-order sensitive",
              "probe: two facts with values {\"x\":1,\"y\":2} and {\"y\":2,\"x\":1} for the same requirement produced Status CONFLICTING, though the values are semantically equal"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F4",
            "clusterKey": "twin-fact-value-byte-sensitivity",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/twin.go:289",
            "summary": "NewTemporalFact derives the content-addressed fact ID from the marshaled struct including the byte-exact Value, so semantically equal facts with different JSON key order yield different fact IDs, fragmenting identity for identical observations.",
            "confidence": "9/10",
            "evidence": [
              "fact identity is byte-sensitive, not value-canonical (no JCS/canonical JSON normalization of Value)",
              "probe: NewTemporalFact on the same logical value with keys in two orders produced id1 != id2"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F5",
            "clusterKey": "costmodel-validation-skipped-when-no-item-costs",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/scheduler.go:191",
            "summary": "When a Problem supplies a CostModel but no item carries EstimatedCostMicros (hasCosts==false), validate() skips CostModel currency/source/asOf checks entirely, so a malformed CostModel (bad currency, empty source, non-RFC3339 asOf) is accepted and echoed into Result.CostModel.",
            "confidence": "8/10",
            "evidence": [
              "the Result then carries an unvalidated cost model that downstream cost-evidence checks (MeasureCostAccuracy) never re-validate for well-formedness",
              "validate() only enforces CostModel fields inside `if hasCosts {...}`; a probe with CostModel{Currency:\"bad\",Source:\"\",AsOf:\"not-a-time\"} and costless items returned err=nil and the malformed CostModel in the Result"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F6",
            "clusterKey": "cost-tiebreak-treats-unknown-as-worst",
            "category": "documentation",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/scheduler.go:300",
            "summary": "On an exact load tie with optimizeCost enabled, a candidate with a KNOWN cost is preferred over one whose cost is UNKNOWN (`cheaper := optimizeCost \u0026\u0026 candidateKnown \u0026\u0026 (!bestKnown || ...)`), so a missing estimate is effectively treated as worst-case for placement even though the doc states only that a missing estimate 'suppresses aggregate cost' and is 'not treated as free'; the tie-break direction is undocumented.",
            "confidence": "6/10",
            "evidence": [
              "ADVISORY-OPTIMIZATION.md says 'a missing estimate remains unknown and suppresses aggregate cost instead of being treated as free' but does not state that unknown cost loses ties to any known cost",
              "round() sets cheaper true when candidateKnown \u0026\u0026 !bestKnown; a probe with one known-cost and one unknown-cost equal-capacity resource assigned the item to the known-cost resource on the tie"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F7",
            "clusterKey": "evaluator-snapshot-binding-not-executable",
            "category": "provenance",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/evaluator.go:174",
            "summary": "The binding between an EvaluatorRun and the immutable git snapshot is only format-checked: validateEvaluatorRun requires InputSnapshotID to match `snap_`+64-hex, but no code recomputes the platform/ archive digest or cross-checks it against a receipt produced by build-evaluator-snapshot.sh, so a run can claim any well-formed snap_ ID that does not correspond to the audited commit.",
            "confidence": "7/10",
            "evidence": [
              "build-evaluator-snapshot.sh emits snap_\u003csha256-of-tar\u003e, but there is no executable gate linking a recorded EvaluatorRun.InputSnapshotID to that receipt; git grep shows InputSnapshotID is only ever format-validated",
              "validateEvaluatorRun calls only validContentID(run.InputSnapshotID, \"snap_\") (length/prefix/hex), never hashing the tree or comparing to build-evaluator-snapshot.sh output"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          },
          {
            "id": "kimi-F8",
            "clusterKey": "dora-window-duration-overflow-saturation",
            "category": "correctness",
            "target": "platform/internal/optimization/dora.go:52",
            "summary": "AssessDORA computes windowDuration = windowEnd.Sub(windowStart) and later uses windowDuration.Nanoseconds() as a big.Rat denominator and WindowSeconds; for windows approaching/exceeding the int64 duration range (~292 years) time.Time.Sub saturates and Nanoseconds() can overflow, so WindowSeconds/DeploymentFrequencyPerSecond silently misreport for extreme windows with no guard.",
            "confidence": "4/10",
            "evidence": [
              "a probe at 290 years produced a finite WindowSeconds (9145440000) with no error; there is no upper-bound check on the window such as exists for MaximumDurationSec in experiment plans",
              "dora.go: `windowDuration := windowEnd.Sub(windowStart)`, `exactDurationSeconds(windowDuration)`, and `SetFrac(..., big.NewInt(windowDuration.Nanoseconds()))` use the saturated int64 nanosecond count"
            ],
            "hypothesis": true
          }
        ]
      }
